### What are the two parts of the microkernel?

The core system contains the minimal functionality and the machinery to discover, register, and invoke plugins. It is deliberately small and stable. The plugins are independent components that each implement a well-defined contract.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public interface ProductPlugin {

    /** Does this plugin handle the given product type? */
    boolean supports(String productType);

    /** Compute the terms for an application. */
    ProductTerms computeTerms(LoanApplication application);

    /** Human-readable name for logging and audit. */
    String getName();
}
```

### How does the core use the plugin contract?

The core holds a registry and delegates to the matching plugin. No giant switch statement.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ProductEngine {

    private final List<ProductPlugin> plugins = new ArrayList<>();

    public void register(ProductPlugin plugin) {
        plugins.add(plugin);
        System.out.println("Registered plugin: " + plugin.getName());
    }

    public ProductTerms process(LoanApplication application) {
        for (ProductPlugin plugin : plugins) {
            if (plugin.supports(application.getProductType())) {
                return plugin.computeTerms(application);
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "No plugin found for: " + application.getProductType());
    }
}
```

### How do you implement a concrete plugin?

Each product is a plugin implementing the contract.

```java
package com.bank.products;

import com.bank.core.ProductPlugin;
import com.bank.core.ProductTerms;
import com.bank.core.LoanApplication;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class HomeLoanPlugin implements ProductPlugin {

    @Override
    public boolean supports(String productType) {
        return "HOME_LOAN".equals(productType);
    }

    @Override
    public ProductTerms computeTerms(LoanApplication app) {
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("8.50"); // 8.5% annual
        int tenureMonths = 240; // 20 years
        BigDecimal emi = calculateEmi(app.getAmount(), rate, tenureMonths);
        return new ProductTerms(rate, tenureMonths, emi);
    }

    private BigDecimal calculateEmi(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal annualRate,
                                     int months) {
        // Simplified EMI calculation
        BigDecimal monthlyRate = annualRate.divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(1200), 10,
            java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
        return principal.multiply(monthlyRate); // simplified
    }

    @Override
    public String getName() { return "Home Loan Plugin"; }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.products;

import com.bank.core.ProductPlugin;
import com.bank.core.ProductTerms;
import com.bank.core.LoanApplication;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FixedDepositPlugin implements ProductPlugin {

    @Override
    public boolean supports(String productType) {
        return "FIXED_DEPOSIT".equals(productType);
    }

    @Override
    public ProductTerms computeTerms(LoanApplication app) {
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("7.25");
        int tenureMonths = 12;
        BigDecimal maturityAmount = app.getAmount()
            .multiply(BigDecimal.ONE.add(rate.divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(100))));
        return new ProductTerms(rate, tenureMonths, maturityAmount);
    }

    @Override
    public String getName() { return "Fixed Deposit Plugin"; }
}
```

### How do you discover plugins dynamically?

Java's `ServiceLoader` discovers implementations on the classpath without recompiling the core.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ServiceLoader;

public class PluginDiscovery {

    public static void loadPlugins(ProductEngine engine) {
        ServiceLoader<ProductPlugin> loader = ServiceLoader.load(ProductPlugin.class);
        for (ProductPlugin plugin : loader) {
            engine.register(plugin);
        }
    }

    // META-INF/services/com.bank.core.ProductPlugin contains:
    // com.bank.products.HomeLoanPlugin
    // com.bank.products.FixedDepositPlugin
    //
    // Adding a new product: write the class, add to the services file.
    // Core is never touched.
}
```

### How do you put it all together?

Register plugins and process applications.

```java
package com.bank;

import com.bank.core.*;
import com.bank.products.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BankingApp {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ProductEngine engine = new ProductEngine();
        engine.register(new HomeLoanPlugin());
        engine.register(new FixedDepositPlugin());

        LoanApplication app = new LoanApplication(
            "CUST-RAJESH-001", "HOME_LOAN", new BigDecimal("5000000"));
        ProductTerms terms = engine.process(app);

        System.out.println("Product: " + app.getProductType());
        System.out.println("Rate: " + terms.getRate() + "%");
        System.out.println("Tenure: " + terms.getTenureMonths() + " months");
        System.out.println("EMI: " + terms.getAmount());
    }
}
```

```text
Registered plugin: Home Loan Plugin
Registered plugin: Fixed Deposit Plugin
Product: HOME_LOAN
Rate: 8.50%
Tenure: 240 months
EMI: 35416.6666666666
```

### Where does the microkernel architecture fit?

| System | Core | Plugins |
|---|---|---|
| IDE (IntelliJ, VS Code) | Editor, file management | Language support, debuggers, themes |
| CMS (WordPress) | Content engine, routing | Content types, workflows, widgets |
| Payment processor | Transaction engine | Card, UPI, net banking, wallet |
| Rules engine | Rule evaluator | Domain-specific rules |
| Banking product engine | Ledger, customer, KYC | Home loan, FD, credit card plugins |

### What are the trade-offs and senior nuances?

Designing the plugin contract well is the most important and hardest decision. Versioning requires care.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public interface ProductPlugin {

    boolean supports(String productType);
    ProductTerms computeTerms(LoanApplication application);
    String getName();

    // Contract evolution: add default method for backward compatibility
    default boolean isEligible(String customerId) {
        return true; // existing plugins do not break
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.core.antipattern;

// WRONG: changing the plugin contract without a default method
// All existing plugins must be updated simultaneously.
// Defeats the purpose of the architecture.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you handle plugin dependencies (plugin A needs plugin B)? How do you sandbox third-party plugins? How do you test plugins in isolation? What is the difference between microkernel architecture and microservices? How do you handle cross-plugin communication?
